<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="QUM98wUlg3vjXlxixsFmr1" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="SIMDywDLwNbQ5UdOPHQxYD" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Sm17wEPgUJCVB75op3gzPF" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="CCoTJSCcdlnfZwmjUnPFVB" Location="VL.Mapsui" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="FJzZkKqVfW12i9kQk6L0hz">
    <Canvas Id="N0E8bKG5vqG1aLaGsfcogC" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="Pt4eKK7Ag0dikcitT4Vmar">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="T1yChjTcVpcQLJ4rxMEL44">
        <Canvas Id="VxYLn5bECvkjhyGerEE3sc" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="VaZNqihOnpwz6dORWEdpu0" Bounds="60,30,620,160" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="01 - Change how the world looks.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;ONE KNOB, AND IT IS AN INTEGER. Everything this map is made of - every road, every coastline, every&#xD;&#xA;colour - arrives as small square pictures from a server. WHICH server is a preset, and Basemap picks&#xD;&#xA;it. Turn it and the whole world changes appearance. Nothing else in this patch has to move.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  0  OpenTopoMap     contours and hillshade&#xD;&#xA;  1  OpenStreetMap   the one everybody knows&#xD;&#xA;  2  CyclOSM         built for street level - zoom IN before judging this one&#xD;&#xA;  3  Satellite       Sentinel-2, a whole year with the clouds taken out&#xD;&#xA;  4  Night lights    NASA Black Marble. Cities are the only thing left&#xD;&#xA;  5  Terrain Light   almost nothing: land, water, relief&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Switch Enabled on first. It starts off because opening a document in vvvv RUNS it, and a map that&#xD;&#xA;fetched the moment you opened it would give you no chance to decline.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Out-of-range is safe: Switch is built on GetSlice, which wraps. Type 9 and you get preset 3.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE CREDIT TRAVELS WITH THE TILES, and that is the reason for the second Switch. Almost every tile&#xD;&#xA;service requires credit ON THE MAP, and a layer carrying none contributes nothing to the Attribution&#xD;&#xA;widget. Two Switches on one Index means the credit cannot be left behind on the wrong basemap -&#xD;&#xA;which is easy to do, entirely invisible, and turns complying into appearing to.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;If you add a seventh preset, ADD IT TO BOTH. A missing pin gives an empty string in silence. When a&#xD;&#xA;list gets long enough for that to be a real risk, the answer is a record with a Url field and an&#xD;&#xA;Attribution field, so the two cannot be separated at all - VL.Mapsui&apos;s HowTo Draw many features&#xD;&#xA;builds one.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO BASEMAPS THAT WORK PERFECTLY AND ARE NOT ALLOWED, which is the more useful lesson: CARTO&apos;s&#xD;&#xA;(their own licence restricts the tile service to enterprise customers) and Esri&apos;s World Imagery&#xD;&#xA;(granted only for tracing features). Both return a correct, good-looking tile if you ask. A REQUEST&#xD;&#xA;THAT SUCCEEDS TELLS YOU NOTHING ABOUT WHETHER YOU WERE ALLOWED TO MAKE IT.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Presets 3, 4 and 5 come from EOX&apos;s demo service, which rate-limits and offers no guarantee. Being a&#xD;&#xA;good guest is why the cache below matters.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT TO WATCH:&#xD;&#xA;  Layers Built  should go up by ONE each time you change the preset, then stop. It counts rebuilds.&#xD;&#xA;                If it climbs frame after frame without you touching anything, CLOSE VVVV - every&#xD;&#xA;                rebuild starts a fresh round of tile requests, and that once exhausted a machine of&#xD;&#xA;                ephemeral ports and took a home network down.&#xD;&#xA;  Cache Status  where tiles are kept, and how much. Each basemap now gets its OWN folder in there.&#xD;&#xA;                It did not until 2026-08-17, and the bug was found by this very patch: with one&#xD;&#xA;                shared folder the second basemap you picked never fetched anything, because the&#xD;&#xA;                first one&apos;s tiles were already sitting under the same {zoom}/{x}/{y} name. Changing&#xD;&#xA;                the preset appeared to do nothing at all, and every status pin said it had worked.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THIS PATCH USES ONE PACKAGE, VL.Mapsui, which is normally a reason to live in that package&apos;s own&#xD;&#xA;help folder rather than here. It is the exemption written into this pack&apos;s rules: a course with a&#xD;&#xA;hole at chapter 1 is worse than a node appearing in two places.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE TO GO NEXT - three different directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 02                 put your own shapes on it&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt Two colours only     how little can a map be made of and still read?&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt Out of this world    no basemap at all. Invent the place.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="I2JQYurLjzub03mQOSqnjh" Comment="Basemap" Bounds="-1500,300,40,26" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="0">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="Integer32" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
